WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:04.380
Stroll down the cereal aisle at any grocery
store and you'll find a myriad of choices

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.790
for starting your day.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:12.360
Healthy and unhealthy, packed with sugar or
packed with bran, we're not wanting for options.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:17.099
The idea of a breakfast cereal industry is
one that's taken for granted in today's modern

00:00:17.099 --> 00:00:21.720
world, but it's a surprisingly recent idea
with a bizarre history.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.689
Kellogg is, of course, one of the first names
that comes to mind when the conversation turns

00:00:25.689 --> 00:00:26.689
to breakfast.

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:29.119
While official Kellogg's history notes that
it was W.K.

00:00:29.119 --> 00:00:33.760
Kellogg who founded the company first known
as the Battle Creek Toasted Corn Flake Company

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:39.370
— and who hired their first set of 44 employees
— it was his brother, Dr. John Harvey Kellogg,

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.660
who was the driving force behind the development
of breakfast cereal.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:47.520
And it's a tale they definitely don't tell
on the back of any cereal box.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.059
There's devout religious teachings, a condemnation
of sex and fulfillment of natural urges, a

00:00:53.059 --> 00:00:58.600
deep-seated distrust of conventional medicines,
and a faith in radical treatments that today

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.470
seem nothing less than barbaric.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.909
On one hand, Kellog was driven by a need to
make the world a better, healthier place.

00:01:05.909 --> 00:01:10.979
But on the other hand, he also dabbled in
things like eugenics and promoted the idea

00:01:10.979 --> 00:01:14.420
of sterilizing people he deemed undesireable.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:18.960
He was a complicated individual, showing us
that the world — and indeed, history — is

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.829
full of people who do the wrong things for
the right reasons.

00:01:22.829 --> 00:01:26.621
And sometimes, an entire business empire is
built on their shoulders.

00:01:26.621 --> 00:01:39.530
The beliefs that built breakfast

00:01:39.530 --> 00:01:43.640
To truly understand who John Harvey Kellogg
was, we have to start with his parents.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.240
John Preston Kellogg and Anne Stanley were
farmers, but they were also devout individuals

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:51.680
who were living on the cusp of the foundation
of a new religion.

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:56.399
In 1852, they were introduced to the beliefs
of a new religious movement that was spawned

00:01:56.399 --> 00:02:03.330
from events that took place — or, more accurately,
didn't take place on October 22, 1844.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:07.409
That was an important date for a group called
the Millerites, and they believed it was the

00:02:07.409 --> 00:02:09.990
day of a very literal Second Coming.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:14.300
They believed Christ was going to return to
walk the earth once again, and when he didn't,

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:16.720
the date became known as the Great Disappointment.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:21.330
Religion is, of course, complicated and difficult
to sum up quickly, but the gist of the story

00:02:21.330 --> 00:02:25.750
is that Christ's apparent failure to return
to earth divided followers.

00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:30.000
Some followed a woman named Ellen Harmon,
who claimed to have experienced a prophetic

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.220
event in December of that same year.

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:35.970
She said it had been revealed to her that
the date was right, and she had been given

00:02:35.970 --> 00:02:41.640
a sign they needed to organize and travel
the straight and narrow path… right into

00:02:41.640 --> 00:02:42.640
Heaven.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:46.560
The group was following a set of rather disorganized
beliefs and teachings when the Kelloggs were

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.480
introduced to the movement, and it wasn't
until 1863 that they organized into the Seventh-day

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:53.480
Adventist church.

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:57.230
By then, the Kelloggs were already well established
in the movement, having helped relocate the

00:02:57.230 --> 00:02:58.820
group to Battle Creek, Michigan.

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:04.760
John Harvey Kellogg was born in 1852, and
was front and center in the burgeoning Seventh-day

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:05.760
Adventist movement.

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:09.910
When he was just 12 years old, co-founder
James White — who had married the prophetic

00:03:09.910 --> 00:03:14.590
Ellen Harmon — invited the young Kellogg
to learn all he could about the printing trade,

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:19.200
with the ultimate goal of getting him to aid
in the spread of their teachings via the printed

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:20.200
word.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:24.340
Kellogg was fascinated by both the teachings
and writings of the new movement, but also

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.820
by other works.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:31.190
Earlier health reformers had been preaching
things like temperance, abstinence, and vegetarianism

00:03:31.190 --> 00:03:35.940
all in the name of living a life that would
bring the mortal human closer to God, and

00:03:35.940 --> 00:03:40.070
that fell in line with what the Adventists
were just beginning to teach.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:44.500
That was the beginning of Kellogg's rise to
the attention of Adventist leaders, and in

00:03:44.500 --> 00:03:49.110
order to make sense of what comes later in
his life, it's important to focus on where

00:03:49.110 --> 00:03:52.610
he was coming from and what beliefs he was
building on.

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:57.620
Seventh-day Adventist tradition says that
people have a moral obligation to lead a healthy

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:58.620
life.

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:02.230
Taking care of the physical form, they say,
is the only way to guarantee complete service

00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:07.660
to God, and living the best, healthiest life
possible includes things like forgoing alcohol

00:04:07.660 --> 00:04:08.660
and tobacco.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:11.870
But there was a bit more to it in Kellogg's
day.

00:04:11.870 --> 00:04:15.920
Adventist leaders were critical of conventional
medicine, and wanted to train their own group

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:21.400
of doctors who would have beliefs rooted in
Adventism, but experience in practical medicine

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:25.510
that would allow them to be critical while
speaking from a well-informed position.

00:04:25.510 --> 00:04:30.500
So, Kellogg was recruited as one of a group
of people sent to study first at the Hygieo-Therapeutic

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:34.820
College in New Jersey, then later at the University
of Michigan Medical School.

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.710
Kellogg didn't aspire to be a doctor in the
traditional sense; instead of treating illness,

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:40.090
he wanted to help prevent it.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:44.950
He earned his M.D. from New York City's Bellevue
Hospital Medical College in 1875, and by that

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.390
time, he had already published a cookbook
focused on improving dietary habits, authored

00:04:49.390 --> 00:04:54.550
a work on the importance of vegetarianism,
and took a position he would hold until he

00:04:54.550 --> 00:04:59.800
died, as editor of a monthly Adventist periodical
he later named Good Health.

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:04.430
The Battle Creek Sanitarium

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:10.630
In 1876, Kellogg was appointed the superintendent
of a facility called the Western Health Reform

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:14.140
Institute, which was quickly renamed the Battle
Creek Sanitarium.

00:05:14.140 --> 00:05:18.930
Over the next few decades, Kellogg and his
brother, Will, would oversee the development

00:05:18.930 --> 00:05:24.810
of this midwestern facility into a globally-recognized
destination, part resort, part medical facility,

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.470
and part retreat of the rich and powerful.

00:05:27.470 --> 00:05:28.990
That's not an exaggeration, either.

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:33.860
At the height of its popularity, the facility
welcomed between 12,000 and 15,000 new patients

00:05:33.860 --> 00:05:36.880
each year, and some of those faces changed
history.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:40.450
Among the patient list were names like Henry
Ford, Thomas Edison, John D. Rockefeller,

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:43.600
Warren Harding, Booker T. Washington, and
Sojourner Truth.

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.880
Even Amelia Earhart stopped in for a little
R&amp;R before major flights, a practice that

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:50.100
came to be known as "taking the cure".

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:53.370
The facility was impressive, and "impressive"
is putting it mildly.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:58.890
In addition to a 15-story tower with 1200
bedrooms, there were hundreds of baths, a

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:03.170
lobby the size of a football field, indoor
gardens, and to give an idea of the scale

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:07.440
of the place, there were a full five acres
of marble-covered floors.

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.270
The sanitarium had its own power plant, a
staff of doctors, nurses, orderlies, masseuses,

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:16.650
and at the head of it all was the good Dr.
Kellogg, who met with each and every patient

00:06:16.650 --> 00:06:21.280
who got off the train and was delivered into
his care by liveried coachmen.

00:06:21.280 --> 00:06:26.280
It sounds absolutely brilliant, but here's
where things start to get… weird.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:30.350
Kellogg's sanitarium was outfitted with all
the equipment he could possibly need to get

00:06:30.350 --> 00:06:35.139
patients in tip-top shape, but treatments
weren't necessarily ones people were thrilled

00:06:35.139 --> 00:06:36.430
to be prescribed.

00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:41.170
One of the most popular rooms was the sanctum
sanctorum, which was filled with — and yes,

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.130
this was a real thing — enema machines.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:49.760
Each machine was capable of pumping 15 quarts
of water you-know-where in just a single minute,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:54.610
and if there's anything that will encourage
you to reach for that high-fibre cereal, it's

00:06:54.610 --> 00:06:55.610
that.

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:59.280
In extreme cases where that wasn't enough
to get patients having their recommended 4

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:03.520
bowel movements per day — a number Kellogg
reached after watching apes — they were

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:04.520
prescribed yogurt.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:09.100
First, a pint of yogurt per day was added
to a patient's diet, then they were given

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.639
a yogurt enema.

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:13.520
Why?

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.400
Cleanliness.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:16.770
Kellogg was obsessed with it.

00:07:16.770 --> 00:07:18.630
Take those marble floors.

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:22.889
They weren't there for appearances, they were
there because they were a surface where "germs

00:07:22.889 --> 00:07:25.010
and vermin can never find a lodging."

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:29.780
Enemas weren't the only commonly prescribed
treatment patients could expect to experience.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.770
Kellogg was also a proponent of light therapy,
and he was the inventor of something called

00:07:33.770 --> 00:07:35.470
the electric light bath.

00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:39.350
Essentially, patients were put into a cabinet
lit with light bulbs, and it was thought to

00:07:39.350 --> 00:07:43.040
be a cure for a number of ailments, everything
from writer's cramp to syphilis.

00:07:43.040 --> 00:07:47.590
And it's also worth noting that this one isn't
as far-fetched as it seems at first glance

00:07:47.590 --> 00:07:53.100
— some of Kellogg's work laid the groundwork
in using light therapy to treat depression.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:07:56.590
Kellogg was also interested in the healing
powers of electricity, and prescribed regular

00:07:56.590 --> 00:08:01.570
sinusoidal current treatments to some patients,
particularly those diagnosed with tuberculosis

00:08:01.570 --> 00:08:02.570
and lead poisoning.

00:08:02.570 --> 00:08:06.170
The mild electrical currents were applied
with the help of a device he made himself

00:08:06.170 --> 00:08:10.440
from a telephone, and sometimes the current
was applied to the skin, and sometimes, it

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.860
was applied straight to the eyeballs in an
attempt to treat ocular disorders.

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:16.820
And that wasn't the only machine he invented.

00:08:16.820 --> 00:08:21.690
The sanitarium was filled with devices that
would shake, vibrate, slap, and even beat

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.430
patients to various ends.

00:08:23.430 --> 00:08:27.910
Some were designed to stimulate the bowels,
others were thought to help improve circulation.

00:08:27.910 --> 00:08:32.380
And some of the good doctor's wealthiest patients
had their own versions of his machines installed

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:36.810
in their homes — Calvin Coolidge even had
one in the White House during his term as

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:37.810
president.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:41.020
It's also worth noting that people loved going
for treatments at the clinic, affectionately

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:42.539
known as the San.

00:08:42.539 --> 00:08:46.160
Just a few years after Kellogg took over,
he went from treating 300 people a year to

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:48.540
1200, and numbers just kept climbing.

00:08:48.540 --> 00:08:52.720
He truly believed in what he was doing, and
even advertised in major magazines to help

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:55.050
get the word out about his miraculous treatments.

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:59.839
An advertisement ran in Good Housekeeping
in 1907, and boasted of the 46 different kinds

00:08:59.839 --> 00:09:01.890
of baths at Kellogg's disposal.

00:09:01.890 --> 00:09:06.170
Some were ordinary, like foot baths, some
were odd, like the light baths, and some could

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.590
only be described as extensive.

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:12.920
Patients complaining of ailments like chronic
diarrhea, various skin conditions, and mental

00:09:12.920 --> 00:09:15.750
illnesses might be prescribed a continuous
bath.

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:16.860
And what was that?

00:09:16.860 --> 00:09:21.600
It was a normal bath, taken in a tub, but
a patient could be instructed to stay there

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:23.210
for days, or even months.

00:09:23.210 --> 00:09:26.890
The more you think about that, the worse it
gets, but it wasn't the worst thing Kellogg

00:09:26.890 --> 00:09:31.980
ever prescribed — that dubious honor belongs
to his cures for what he called "the solitary

00:09:31.980 --> 00:09:32.980
vice".

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:37.910
Mastubation, he believed, would ultimately
lead to things like heart disease and insanity,

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:41.120
so to stop patients with a chronic habit,
he did terrible things.

00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:45.500
Boys would be bound, tied, or forced to wear
cages, and if that didn't work, they were

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.930
circumcised without anesthetic in hopes that
the pain would discourage further acts.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:54.390
Girls were not exempt, either, and some were
burned with acid or subjected to surgery to

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:56.140
remove part of the genitals.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:10:00.710
Kellogg was devoted to health and godliness,
and he was extreme.

00:10:00.710 --> 00:10:04.770
Amid these cures, Kellogg also focused on
something that he believed desperately needed

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:07.790
an overhaul: breakfast.

00:10:07.790 --> 00:10:12.839
Changing the way America ate

00:10:12.839 --> 00:10:17.260
This is where we go back to the heart of Kellogg's
Seventh-day Adventist beliefs.

00:10:17.260 --> 00:10:21.800
Co-founder Ellen Harmon White wrote that the
truly devout would follow a vegetarian diet,

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:24.850
because fruit and vegetables were seen as
God's gift to mankind.

00:10:24.850 --> 00:10:29.210
She taught that people should eat foods that
could be consumed in their natural state:

00:10:29.210 --> 00:10:31.750
fruits, vegetables, nuts, and grains.

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:35.779
Kellogg followed that belief, at a time when
breakfast was about as unhealthy as you can

00:10:35.779 --> 00:10:36.779
imagine.

00:10:36.779 --> 00:10:40.190
Kellogg, remember, was living at a time when
most people started their day with the previous

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:41.589
nights' leftovers.

00:10:41.589 --> 00:10:45.860
Some of the most common breakfasts involved
meat and potatoes reheated in a pan with whatever

00:10:45.860 --> 00:10:47.290
fats they had congealed in.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:51.440
Heavy meals of salted and cured meats were
also popular, and anything grain-based was

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:52.440
difficult.

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:56.320
It was time-consuming and labor-intensive,
and it meant getting up before the sun, stirring

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.770
and cooking raw grains into gruel.

00:10:58.770 --> 00:11:04.160
In 1858, Walt Whitman wrote that indigestion
was "the great American evil," and it's no

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:05.160
wonder.

00:11:05.160 --> 00:11:09.380
Gastrointestinal distress was so common and
so frequent there was one catch-all term used

00:11:09.380 --> 00:11:12.730
to describe all the symptoms: dyspepsia.

00:11:12.730 --> 00:11:16.060
Kellogg's attempts at overhauling breakfast
were practical: he wanted something that was

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:19.520
easy for someone to prepare, and just as easy
on the stomach.

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:23.279
He and his brother spent years experimenting
in their own food lab, trying to come up with

00:11:23.279 --> 00:11:28.320
vegetarian diets that would be easy for any
patient to digest, and that were boring.

00:11:28.320 --> 00:11:29.990
The boring part was on purpose.

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:34.240
Part of the theory was that bland, boring
food would not only keep their patients walking

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:38.640
the straight and narrow right up into heaven
by consuming food in the way God meant, but

00:11:38.640 --> 00:11:42.690
also that it would prevent them from getting
too excited and overstimulated.

00:11:42.690 --> 00:11:45.310
They did have some success, starting with
protose.

00:11:45.310 --> 00:11:50.100
It was essentially an early version of a veggie
burger, tasted of peanuts, and was both given

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:53.050
to sanitarium patients and sold through mail
order.

00:11:53.050 --> 00:11:57.470
He also developed something he called "granula",
until another 19th century foodie claimed

00:11:57.470 --> 00:12:00.360
the rights to it, and forced him to change
the name.

00:12:00.360 --> 00:12:04.180
Kellogg's granula — bars of corn meal and
oatmeal that were baked then ground into bits

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:06.200
— became known as granola.

00:12:06.200 --> 00:12:09.390
Just how the Kellogg brothers made their leap
into breakfast cereal is a bit of legend and

00:12:09.390 --> 00:12:13.610
a bit of history, and the story goes that
they mistakenly left wheat-berry dough on

00:12:13.610 --> 00:12:14.710
the counter overnight.

00:12:14.710 --> 00:12:18.190
It was stale the next morning, and when they
rolled it out and toasted it, they got some

00:12:18.190 --> 00:12:21.279
crunchy flakes that became a huge hit with
patients.

00:12:21.279 --> 00:12:24.830
Kellogg handed the marketing of their breakfast
foods off to his brother, who was working

00:12:24.830 --> 00:12:26.550
as the sanitarium's bookkeeper.

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:29.330
But all was not well at the Battle Creek Sanitarium.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:34.089
Debts were mounting, church elders were concerned
by a curriculum they saw as getting far away

00:12:34.089 --> 00:12:38.440
from their Seventh-day Adventist roots, and
when a fire swept through the compound they

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.260
told him they wouldn't be rebuilding.

00:12:40.260 --> 00:12:44.440
At the same time, Kellogg's brother, Will,
was pushing a practical way of getting more

00:12:44.440 --> 00:12:46.279
people to buy their corn flakes.

00:12:46.279 --> 00:12:49.720
He wanted to add sugar, an addition that Kellogg
found abhorrent.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:53.620
Will left to found the Battle Creek Toasted
Corn Flake Company, and Kellogg tried his

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:56.540
own hand at selling the flakes they'd invented
together.

00:12:56.540 --> 00:13:01.170
Will sued, won, and went the business route
while John Harvey Kellogg stayed on at the

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:02.170
San.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.020
It's also worth a quick aside here to say
that Kellogg's wasn't the only cereal company

00:13:05.020 --> 00:13:06.459
to grow out of this era.

00:13:06.459 --> 00:13:10.160
One of the Kellogg's recurring patients was
a man named Charles William Post, who stayed

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:14.840
at the sanitarium in the 1890s during periods
of mental breakdowns and stress.

00:13:14.840 --> 00:13:18.390
He saw what they were doing, picked up on
the trend, and went on to sell the cereals

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:21.060
Postum and Grape-Nuts through his own company.

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:24.890
Post's company is generally credited as being
the force that pushed Kellogg to develop better

00:13:24.890 --> 00:13:29.399
products with a longer shelf life, and as
for the competition, Kellogg would comment

00:13:29.399 --> 00:13:34.860
that he "was not after the business; I am
after the reform; that is what I want to see."

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:40.130
Kellogg held his patients to strict rules,
and that included sticking to his bland, vegetarian

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.910
diet and a requirement that they chew: a lot.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:48.019
A devotee of Horace Fletcher, he prescribed
to the theories of Fletcherism that dictated

00:13:48.019 --> 00:13:51.250
chewing each bite of food 40 times would aid
in digestion.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:55.920
He was often heard leading diners in singing
his "Chewing Song," and there's a bit of a

00:13:55.920 --> 00:13:56.920
footnote to this.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.940
Not all of his patients were happy with the
diet and the philosophy of no alcohol and

00:14:00.940 --> 00:14:01.940
no smoking.

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:05.050
Fortunately for them, a particularly brilliant
entrepreneur set up a place just down the

00:14:05.050 --> 00:14:10.520
street, called it the Red Onion Tavern, and
served steaks, cigars, and whiskey to patients…

00:14:10.520 --> 00:14:14.890
along with a promise to get them back out
the door in time to beat the sanitarium's

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:18.070
11 pm curfew.

00:14:18.070 --> 00:14:22.330
Life beyond the sanitarium

00:14:22.330 --> 00:14:25.670
While Kellogg spent much of his life working
at the Battle Creek Sanitarium, meeting with

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:30.000
patients while wearing a distinctive — and
clean — white suit with a cockatoo on his

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:33.980
shoulder, there was more to his life than
just a never-ending parade of patients.

00:14:33.980 --> 00:14:38.960
He married Ella Eaton in 1879, and while they
had no biological children they fostered more

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:41.649
than 40 kids and officially adopted several.

00:14:41.649 --> 00:14:46.000
She played an invaluable role in his dietary
experiments, and she also introduced him to

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:50.050
new social spheres of influence, like the
Woman's Christian Temperance Union.

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:54.410
He was also an adept surgeon, spending some
time studying abdominal surgeries in London

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:55.410
and Vienna.

00:14:55.410 --> 00:14:59.680
By the time he finally put down his surgeon's
scalpel at 88 years of age, he had performed

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:02.160
around 22,000 procedures.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:06.209
Kellogg had a falling-out with his Adventist
church leaders in 1907, and was ultimately

00:15:06.209 --> 00:15:07.370
expelled from the church.

00:15:07.370 --> 00:15:11.370
They believed that he was using church funds
for medical research and education instead

00:15:11.370 --> 00:15:15.080
of evangelism, and it was an affront they
could not stand for.

00:15:15.080 --> 00:15:20.120
Kellogg still kept control of the sanitarium,
though, and in addition to remaining a consistent,

00:15:20.120 --> 00:15:24.850
controlling presence there, he also expanded
his reach to open schools for hygiene, nursing,

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:28.650
home economics, and physical education on
sanitarium grounds.

00:15:28.650 --> 00:15:33.060
He served on the Michigan State Board of Health,
wrote around 50 books on the subject of health,

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:37.370
issued some of the first warnings on the dangers
of smoking, and this is about to take a strange

00:15:37.370 --> 00:15:43.470
twist, because he went on to co-found something
less-than-admirable: the Race Betterment Foundation.

00:15:43.470 --> 00:15:48.300
That was exactly what it sounds like: an organization
dedicated toward maintaining the superiority

00:15:48.300 --> 00:15:50.350
of certain races by selective breeding.

00:15:50.350 --> 00:15:53.140
Remember all those children that he and his
wife fostered?

00:15:53.140 --> 00:15:58.350
It wasn't entirely out of the goodness of
his heart, as the Eugenics Archives notes

00:15:58.350 --> 00:16:02.870
that most of the children were, in one way
or another, deemed "undesirables".

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:07.220
Kellogg was hoping to make some sort of discovery
on the influence of environment on heredity,

00:16:07.220 --> 00:16:09.790
and don't worry, it gets even worse.

00:16:09.790 --> 00:16:13.920
The Battle Creek Sanitarium was at the very
center of the spread of eugenics throughout

00:16:13.920 --> 00:16:18.839
the US during the early 20th century, and
Kellogg devoted about 30 years of his life

00:16:18.839 --> 00:16:23.630
(and a ton of his own money) to the idea that
excluding people with certain genes from the

00:16:23.630 --> 00:16:28.010
pool of breeding humans would improve humanity
as a whole.

00:16:28.010 --> 00:16:29.010
Exclude them how?

00:16:29.010 --> 00:16:32.570
While he was serving on the Michigan State
Board of Health, he successfully lobbied to

00:16:32.570 --> 00:16:37.730
pass a law that would call for the "sterilization
of mentally defective persons", a law that

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:43.310
led to the involuntary sterilization of at
least 3,800 people in the state.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:47.480
Kellogg — and other believers — preached
that encouraging only certain people to reproduce

00:16:47.480 --> 00:16:54.089
would end all the ills the human race suffered
from, from poverty to criminality to, quote,

00:16:54.089 --> 00:16:55.089
"feeblemindedness".

00:16:55.089 --> 00:17:00.589
That superior race was, of course, the Caucasian
one and yes, there was a very real association

00:17:00.589 --> 00:17:03.620
that would later form with the Nazi party.

00:17:03.620 --> 00:17:07.819
Kellogg began to completely embrace eugenics
after his falling-out with the Seventh-day

00:17:07.819 --> 00:17:11.669
Adventists — a group who, incidentally,
believed that all life was a gift from God.

00:17:11.669 --> 00:17:16.740
He became obsessed with the idea that if things
continued as they were, mankind would begin

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:22.209
to decay and degrade in quality until finally,
it simply ceased to exist.

00:17:22.209 --> 00:17:26.019
In 1914, Kellogg had become good friends with
other leading figures of the eugenics movement,

00:17:26.019 --> 00:17:29.440
like Charles Davenport, founder of the Eugenics
Record Office.

00:17:29.440 --> 00:17:33.769
They held the first Race Betterment Conference
at the San, and one of his guest speakers

00:17:33.769 --> 00:17:38.639
was a former patient — Booker T. Washington
was there to try to talk to attendees and

00:17:38.639 --> 00:17:42.070
convince them that everyone should be treated
fairly and equally.

00:17:42.070 --> 00:17:45.610
The San, incidentally, was always a segregated
facility.

00:17:45.610 --> 00:17:49.781
Conference attendees did more than just examine
and measure around 5,000 children as a part

00:17:49.781 --> 00:17:53.899
of the "Better Babies" contest, they also
made it clear they were going to start pushing

00:17:53.899 --> 00:17:56.159
their ideas further into the US.

00:17:56.159 --> 00:18:01.440
The second conference was held in 1915 at
the Panama-Pacific International Exhibition,

00:18:01.440 --> 00:18:05.919
and it took the society's beliefs — clearly
illustrated by charts highlighting the differences

00:18:05.919 --> 00:18:11.039
between "superior" and "degenerate" races
— and put them squarely in front of 18 million

00:18:11.039 --> 00:18:12.039
visitors.

00:18:12.039 --> 00:18:15.700
He campaigned for the establishment of nationwide
legislature similar to what he had established

00:18:15.700 --> 00:18:20.359
in Michigan, and even proposed a registry
where people would be given "pedigrees," and

00:18:20.359 --> 00:18:25.340
parents who fell within the established guidelines
for "racial hygiene" would be eligible for

00:18:25.340 --> 00:18:26.340
awards.

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:29.590
There were a few things that brought down
the eugenics movement, and the first was the

00:18:29.590 --> 00:18:31.480
extremes of Nazi Germany.

00:18:31.480 --> 00:18:35.190
Even before that, the troubles brought by
the Great Depression proved to many that poverty

00:18:35.190 --> 00:18:39.590
and hardship had nothing to do with genetics,
but eugenics legislation was still in place

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:44.109
— and sterilizations were practiced — into
the 1970s.

00:18:44.109 --> 00:18:46.330
As for Kellogg, he died in 1943.

00:18:46.330 --> 00:19:05.730
He nearly hit the century he had always proclaimed
he would live to: he was 91 years old.

